## 9. API CONVENTIONS

- One handler pattern: resolve input → strict Zod validate → call exactly one service → serialize → respond. No SQL in handlers; no business branching in handlers.
- Success envelope: `{ data, meta?, facets?, rejectedFilters? }`. Error envelope: `{ error: { code, message, details? } }` + `requestId` + `traceId` headers (X-Trace-Id on every response).
- Closed error-code registry (no internals leaked): `VALIDATION_FAILED` 400, `UNAUTHORIZED` 401, `FORBIDDEN` 403, `NOT_FOUND`/`PRODUCT_NOT_FOUND`/`CART_NOT_FOUND`/`ORDER_NOT_FOUND` 404, `METHOD_NOT_ALLOWED` 405, `RATE_LIMITED` 429 (+ RateLimit-* + Retry-After headers), `IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT` 409, `PRICE_CHANGED` 409, `VERSION_CONFLICT` 409, `UNPROCESSABLE` 422 (with domain detail codes like `PAIR_RESERVATION_FAILED`, `REFUND_EXCEEDS_CAPTURED`, `DYO_QUOTE_INVALID`, `CHECKOUT_STEP_SKIPPED`), `INTERNAL` 500, `BAD_GATEWAY` 502.
- Idempotency keys on every mutating endpoint/job/event consumer. Pagination: bounded limits + keyset cursors (deterministic, no overlap). Sorting/filtering whitelisted.
- OpenAPI generated from the same Zod contracts; a parity test proves documented ↔ routed ↔ handled are identical.
- Cache headers from surface policy (public catalogue cacheable with generation-based invalidation; account/commerce `no-store`).

